.txt file content send on responce in node.js


    You can create a Node.js server that reads the content of a .txt file and sends it in the response. Here's a simple example to illustrate how to achieve this:

    Example: Serving a .txt File Content in Response

    Step 1: Create a Sample Text File
    First, create a text file named example.txt in your project directory with some sample content:

    example.txt

    ------------------------------------------
    This is a sample text file.
    It contains multiple lines of text.
    Enjoy reading!

    ------------------------------------------

    Step 2: Create the Server to Serve the Text File Content
    server.js

    -------------------------------------------
    const http = require('http');
    const fs = require('fs');
    const path = require('path');

    const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
        if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/file') {
            const filePath = path.join(__dirname, 'example.txt');

            fs.readFile(filePath, 'utf8', (err, data) => {
                if (err) {
                    res.writeHead(500, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
                    res.end('Internal Server Error');
                } else {
                    res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
                    res.end(data);
                }
            });
        } else {
            res.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
            res.end('404 Not Found');
        }
    });

    // Start the server
    const PORT = 3000;
    server.listen(PORT, () => {
        console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${PORT}/`);
    });

    -------------------------------------------

    Explanation
    Importing Modules:

    ->http: To create the server.

    ->fs: To interact with the file system.

    ->path: To work with file and directory paths.

    ->Creating the Server:

        http.createServer((req, res) => { ... });: Creates an HTTP server and sets up a request handler.

    ->Handling GET Request for the Text File:

    ->Checks if the request method is GET and the URL is /file.

    ->Sets the file path for the text file.

    ->Reading the File:

        fs.readFile(filePath, 'utf8', (err, data) => { ... });: Reads the content of the text file asynchronously.

        If an error occurs (e.g., file not found), it responds with a 500 Internal Server Error status.

        If the file is read successfully, it responds with a 200 OK status and sends the content of the file.

        Handling 404 Not Found:

        If the URL does not match /file, it responds with a 404 Not Found status.

    ->Starting the Server:

    server.listen(PORT, () => { ... });: Starts the server and listens on the specified port.